WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.934
In this lesson you'll learn how old

2
00:00:03.934 --> 00:00:06.047
the tools you build with really are,

3
00:00:06.047 --> 00:00:08.148
and why their age matters.

4
00:00:08.148 --> 00:00:09.878
Almost everything under your AI

5
00:00:09.878 --> 00:00:11.701
tools is decades old,

6
00:00:11.701 --> 00:00:14.766
and only the AI layer on top is new.

7
00:00:14.766 --> 00:00:17.819
The terminal, files, the web,

8
00:00:17.819 --> 00:00:19.921
git: old and settled.

9
00:00:19.921 --> 00:00:23.938
The AI tools: a few years old, and still changing.

10
00:00:23.938 --> 00:00:26.028
So you'll know what to learn deeply once,

11
00:00:26.028 --> 00:00:28.282
and what to keep checking.

12
00:00:28.731 --> 00:00:31.889
On the course map, this lesson sits at the Start,

13
00:00:31.889 --> 00:00:33.538
in Orientation:

14
00:00:33.538 --> 00:00:37.009
what this course is, and the lantern you carry.

15
00:00:37.009 --> 00:00:39.168
Before you walk into the six districts,

16
00:00:39.168 --> 00:00:41.479
it helps to know their ages.

17
00:00:41.479 --> 00:00:43.359
Most of these streets were laid long

18
00:00:43.359 --> 00:00:45.542
before AI tools arrived.

19
00:00:45.542 --> 00:00:49.561
The lanterns, the AI, were strung up last.

20
00:00:50.011 --> 00:00:52.519
Aaron, who made this course, has a degree

21
00:00:52.519 --> 00:00:55.584
in Information Systems and Data Science.

22
00:00:55.584 --> 00:00:57.883
He still wrote Python in Notepad,

23
00:00:57.883 --> 00:01:00.808
and saved it as a .txt file.

24
00:01:00.808 --> 00:01:03.804
He didn't know to rename it to .py,

25
00:01:03.804 --> 00:01:06.729
or to open a terminal and cd into the folder.

26
00:01:06.729 --> 00:01:09.585
He'd never heard of .venv.

27
00:01:09.585 --> 00:01:10.944
None of that was new.

28
00:01:10.944 --> 00:01:14.531
Notepad came with the first Windows, in 1985.

29
00:01:14.531 --> 00:01:16.586
Python is from 1991.

30
00:01:16.586 --> 00:01:19.175
The Unix shell, from 1971.

31
00:01:19.175 --> 00:01:24.086
And the tool behind .venv joined Python in 2012.

32
00:01:24.086 --> 00:01:26.814
Aaron wasn't behind on the latest thing.

33
00:01:26.814 --> 00:01:28.904
He was missing the oldest things,

34
00:01:28.904 --> 00:01:32.435
the ones everyone assumes you already have.

35
00:01:32.886 --> 00:01:34.523
Picture one long street,

36
00:01:34.523 --> 00:01:36.822
with a year painted on every building.

37
00:01:36.822 --> 00:01:39.248
1969: Unix.

38
00:01:39.248 --> 00:01:41.385
1973: Ethernet,

39
00:01:41.385 --> 00:01:44.032
cables joining the computers in one building.

40
00:01:44.032 --> 00:01:46.922
1981: MS-DOS.

41
00:01:46.922 --> 00:01:50.452
1983: DNS, the internet's address book.

42
00:01:50.452 --> 00:01:53.981
Then the web, Python and Linux, all around 1991.

43
00:01:53.981 --> 00:01:55.850
JavaScript, 1995.

44
00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:57.441
Git, 2005.

45
00:01:57.441 --> 00:01:59.171
GitHub, 2008.

46
00:01:59.171 --> 00:02:00.994
Docker, 2013.

47
00:02:00.994 --> 00:02:03.095
VS Code, 2015.

48
00:02:03.095 --> 00:02:04.569
And only at the very end of the street,

49
00:02:04.569 --> 00:02:06.624
the lanterns. GitHub Copilot,

50
00:02:06.624 --> 00:02:09.109
suggesting code in your editor, 2021.

51
00:02:09.109 --> 00:02:11.303
ChatGPT, late 2022.

52
00:02:11.303 --> 00:02:15.169
MCP, a standard way to plug AI into other tools,

53
00:02:15.169 --> 00:02:17.027
November 2024.

54
00:02:17.027 --> 00:02:18.734
More than fifty years of street.

55
00:02:18.734 --> 00:02:20.837
A few years of lanterns.

56
00:02:21.287 --> 00:02:24.085
Lay those years on the road from the last lesson.

57
00:02:24.085 --> 00:02:27.614
Your computer: the shell, files, Python.

58
00:02:27.614 --> 00:02:28.938
Decades old.

59
00:02:28.938 --> 00:02:30.761
The network and the internet:

60
00:02:30.761 --> 00:02:33.628
Ethernet, DNS, the web.

61
00:02:33.628 --> 00:02:34.859
Decades too.

62
00:02:34.859 --> 00:02:38.273
Services like GitHub, Cloudflare and Stripe:

63
00:02:38.273 --> 00:02:39.979
mostly around 2010.

64
00:02:39.979 --> 00:02:43.590
And AI, the last stop, is the newest by far.

65
00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:45.355
Here's where the picture breaks.

66
00:02:45.355 --> 00:02:48.524
Every date is a birthday, not a finish line.

67
00:02:48.524 --> 00:02:51.844
Python and git still get new versions every year;

68
00:02:51.844 --> 00:02:53.957
what's old is the idea.

69
00:02:53.957 --> 00:02:57.185
AI research, too, goes back to the 1950s.

70
00:02:57.185 --> 00:02:59.901
What's new is AI you can talk to,

71
00:02:59.901 --> 00:03:02.364
inside the tools you use.

72
00:03:02.815 --> 00:03:05.706
Knowing the ages explains things you'll meet.

73
00:03:05.706 --> 00:03:09.468
The C:\ at the start of a Windows terminal

74
00:03:09.468 --> 00:03:13.961
is a habit from MS-DOS, in the early 1980s.

75
00:03:13.961 --> 00:03:17.676
A git answer from ten years ago often still works.

76
00:03:17.676 --> 00:03:20.393
That's not luck; the street hasn't moved.

77
00:03:20.393 --> 00:03:23.226
But an old answer where print has no parentheses

78
00:03:23.226 --> 00:03:26.511
is Python 2, retired in 2020.

79
00:03:26.511 --> 00:03:28.636
Same street, older building.

80
00:03:28.636 --> 00:03:31.806
And an AI can be out of date about AI,

81
00:03:31.806 --> 00:03:33.396
because what it learned stops

82
00:03:33.396 --> 00:03:35.033
where its training ended.

83
00:03:35.033 --> 00:03:38.295
So: old street, old answers mostly hold.

84
00:03:38.295 --> 00:03:40.735
Lantern: check the date.

85
00:03:41.185 --> 00:03:42.787
That's why AI tools can feel

86
00:03:42.787 --> 00:03:44.366
strange to learn with.

87
00:03:44.366 --> 00:03:47.629
The newest layer assumes you know the oldest ones.

88
00:03:47.629 --> 00:03:52.632
A coding agent will run cd, write a .py file,

89
00:03:52.632 --> 00:03:57.277
make a .venv, and save its work with git.

90
00:03:57.277 --> 00:03:58.670
If you've never met those,

91
00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:01.213
you can't tell whether it did the right thing.

92
00:04:01.213 --> 00:04:03.012
The newest thing on the road spends

93
00:04:03.012 --> 00:04:05.357
its day on the oldest ground.

94
00:04:05.357 --> 00:04:09.049
AI is the lantern: it lights every district,

95
00:04:09.049 --> 00:04:11.814
but it doesn't walk the streets for you.

96
00:04:12.263 --> 00:04:14.504
You don't need to memorize a single date.

97
00:04:14.504 --> 00:04:17.267
Nobody will quiz you. Who invented what,

98
00:04:17.267 --> 00:04:20.587
and which old system won the argument, can wait.

99
00:04:20.587 --> 00:04:23.200
Keep one question for anything new you meet:

100
00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:25.649
is this street, or lantern?

101
00:04:25.649 --> 00:04:28.354
Street: learn it once.

102
00:04:28.354 --> 00:04:31.467
Lantern: check back often.

103
00:04:31.918 --> 00:04:33.601
Three things to keep.

104
00:04:33.601 --> 00:04:35.981
Most of what you build on is decades old.

105
00:04:35.981 --> 00:04:37.305
The shell, files,

106
00:04:37.305 --> 00:04:39.766
the web and git had decades to settle,

107
00:04:39.766 --> 00:04:41.775
so what you learn there lasts.

108
00:04:41.775 --> 00:04:43.934
The AI layer is brand new.

109
00:04:43.934 --> 00:04:48.822
ChatGPT is from 2022, MCP from 2024,

110
00:04:48.822 --> 00:04:50.180
and both keep changing,

111
00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:52.943
so check the date on anything about them.

112
00:04:52.943 --> 00:04:55.173
Being lost isn't being behind.

113
00:04:55.173 --> 00:04:56.705
Aaron had a degree and still

114
00:04:56.705 --> 00:04:58.226
missed the old basics.

115
00:04:58.226 --> 00:04:59.457
They were never new.

116
00:04:59.457 --> 00:05:01.386
Nobody handed them over.
